## 6. Thermodynamics: Constantin Carathéodory and Reverse Characteristics
If the method of characteristics is about taking a multidimensional surface and breaking it down into individual 1D tracks, Constantin Carathéodory’s formulation of thermodynamics (1909) is about taking individual 1D tracks and gluing them together to manufacture a multidimensional surface.
## Manufacturing the Internal Energy Coordinate ($U$)
In a 2D state space (Pressure $P$ and Volume $V$), work is an inexact differential ($\delta W = -P,dV$). Its cross-derivatives do not match, meaning there is no standalone variable $W$ sitting on the vertices of the mesh.
By physically insulating the system ($\delta Q = 0$), we restrict our movement to adiabatic paths. Carathéodory observed that moving between states exclusively along these adiabatic tracks makes the work done dependent only on the endpoints, independent of the path taken. Along these submanifolds, $\delta W$ behaves like a total differential.
Because it behaves predictably along these tracks, we can use it to anchor a brand new, globally exact coordinate variable onto every vertex of our mesh: Internal Energy, $U$. When we leave the adiabatic tracks, the geometric discrepancy between our new exact coordinate $dU$ and the inexact work step $\delta W$ defines heat:
$$\delta Q = dU - \delta W$$
## The Frobenius Theorem and Entropy ($S$)
To scale this to 3 or more variables (e.g., $P$, $V$, and Chemical Potential $\mu$), Carathéodory invoked the Frobenius Theorem.
## Formal Statement of Frobenius
Let $\omega$ be a smooth, non-vanishing 1-form (like $\delta Q$) on an $n$-dimensional manifold. The local hyperplanes where $\omega = 0$ can be seamlessly glued together into smooth, nested $(n-1)$-dimensional surfaces (foliations) if and only if:
$$\omega \wedge d\omega = 0$$

* In 2D Space: $\omega \wedge d\omega$ tries to form a 3-form. Since a 3-form cannot exist in 2D space, it is identically zero. Thus, 2D tracks can always be glued together to find an integrating factor.
* In 3D Space or Higher: $\omega \wedge d\omega$ measures the local swirl (vorticity) of the tracks. If it is non-zero, the tracks twist like a chaotic whirlpool (a contact structure). A traveler walking strictly along $\omega = 0$ can start at one point and navigate to any nearby point in the room, making it impossible to cleanly glue the lines into separate surfaces.

text IN 2D SPACE: IN 3D SPACE (with high swirl): Tracks are neat, parallel lines. Tracks form a tangled ball of yarn. Can easily glue them to make a surface. Cannot cleanly glue them; lines cross everywhere. 
Carathéodory stated the Second Law as a geometric axiom: In the immediate neighborhood of any state, there are points that are physically unreachable via adiabatic paths ($\delta Q = 0$).
By physically outlawing the chaotic, space-filling swirl, this axiom mathematically forces the algebraic Frobenius condition:
$$\delta Q \wedge d(\delta Q) = 0$$
This guarantees that the multi-dimensional adiabatic tracks flatten out into neat, parallel layers. It proves the existence of a local integrating factor (Temperature, $T$) that cleanly untwists the geometry, yielding the perfectly exact differential of Entropy, $S$:
$$\frac{\delta Q}{T} = dS$$
