## 7. The Invariant $n$-Dimensional Generalization
This true-ratio framework scales seamlessly to any arbitrary number of variables ($x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n$).
To isolate a freestanding partial differential like $\left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_1}\right)_{x_2, \dots, x_n}$, you use your $n-1$ "held constant" constraints to pin down the other coordinates ($dx_2=0, dx_3=0, \dots$). In an $n$-dimensional space, pinning down $n-1$ independent variables leaves you with exactly one remaining degree of freedom—a 1D wire winding through the space.
By introducing an arbitrary parameter $dt$ to drive your steps along this isolated wire, the higher-dimensional problem instantly collapses into a simple 1D Leibnizian ratio of two standard 1-forms:
$$\frac{df/dt}{dx_1/dt} = \frac{df}{dx_1}$$
The arbitrary parameter $dt$ cancels completely, proving the core invariant of this framework: A partial derivative, in any dimension, is simply a 1D Leibnizian true ratio isolated by forcing all other independent coordinate variations to zero.
